Q: What would be a &quot;Hello, World!&quot; example for &quot;std::ref&quot;? A: <p>In the spirit of the original question, I wanted to attempt an answer that does not require knowledge of other std library facilities.</p>
<p>(Note that <code>std::ref(x)</code>, where <code>x</code> is some object of type <code>Foo</code>, is used to create the <code>std::reference_wrapper&lt;Foo&gt;</code> which &quot;wraps&quot; <code>x</code>.)</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;functional&gt;

void incr(int &amp;i) { 
    i++; 
}

// A template function that copies its argument by value
template &lt;typename T&gt;
void proxyCallIncr(T arg) {
    // Upon the second call (where T is reference_wrapper&lt;int&gt;),
    // arg is a copy of the reference_wrapper to x
    // which gets passed as an int&amp; to x into the below call to incr
    incr(arg); 
}

int main() {
    int x = 10;

    // Here, we pass a copy of x (deduces T as int)
    proxyCallIncr(x);
    // x is still 10, because only the copy was incremented
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After normal call: &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

    // Here, we pass a copy of the reference_wrapper (deduces T as reference_wrapper&lt;int&gt;)
    proxyCallIncr(std::ref(x));
    // x has been incremented to 11
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;After std::ref call: &quot; &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Notice also that <code>reference_wrapper&lt;int&gt;</code> is implicitly converted back to <code>int&amp;</code> when calling <code>incr(arg)</code> (equivalent to calling <code>.get()</code> on the <code>reference_wrapper</code>.)</p>

